WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.623
Idea. Human cells are thin, and almost see-through. Under a light microscope, light must pass through them. With no stain, you can hardly see them. A stain is a dye that colours parts of the cell, so they show up clearly.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:29.646
Method. Step one: collect the cells cleanly. Gently rub a sterile cotton bud on the inside of your cheek. Sterile means it has no microbes on it. Microbes are tiny living things, such as bacteria.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:37.067
Step two: smear the cells thinly on a clean slide. A thin layer lets light pass through one layer of cells.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:41.383
Step three: add a drop of stain, such as methylene blue.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:53.777
Step four: lower a coverslip at an angle, so no air bubbles are trapped. A coverslip is a thin square of glass. Then soak up extra stain with a paper towel.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:01:08.621
Step five: put the slide on the stage. Start with the low power objective lens, then focus. For blood, prick a finger with a sterile lancet, a small sharp blade. Then let the smear dry before you stain it.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:23.323
Worked example. Here is a real exam question. The diagram shows two cheek cells. Describe how a student would obtain and prepare these cells, to view them with a light microscope. It is worth four marks.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:33.178
Obtain comes first. Gently rub a sterile cotton bud on the inside of the cheek. Then prepare: smear the cells thinly across a clean slide.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:44.721
Add a drop of methylene blue stain. Lower a coverslip at an angle, and remove extra stain with a paper towel. That is five steps for four marks, which is safe.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:52.243
Here is the full answer. This answer is written by olevels.com. It is not the official mark scheme.

00:01:53.350 --> 00:02:01.579
Common mistake. The most common mistake is to forget the stain. Then the cells are almost see-through, and the student sees nothing.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:13.035
Other mistakes: a thick blob instead of a thin smear, a cotton bud that is not sterile, or putting the slide on the stage before the cells are on it.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:28.090
Your turn. A student wants to look at white blood cells. First, describe how to obtain the blood and prepare the slide. Second, another student saw no cells at all. Suggest why. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:41.483
Answer. Part a. Prick a finger with a sterile lancet. Smear the blood thinly on a clean slide, and let it dry. Add a stain, such as methylene blue. Lower a coverslip at an angle.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:49.988
Part b. The student probably did not add a stain. The cells are almost see-through, so they could not be seen.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:03:02.413
So, every time: collect with something sterile, smear thinly, stain, then add a coverslip at an angle. The slide goes on the stage last.
